### Why is @Transactional not enough for microservices?

A Spring `@Transactional` method wraps a single database connection in one ACID transaction. If it throws, that database rolls back. But in a microservices flow, the Order Service committed to its own database, the Inventory Service reserved stock in its own database, and the Payment Service runs against a third. When Payment fails, your local rollback cannot reach the other services.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class MonolithicOrderService {

    // In a monolith, this works: one transaction, one database
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(String customerId, String productId,
                            BigDecimal amount) {
        orderRepository.createOrder(customerId, productId);   // same DB
        inventoryRepository.reserveStock(productId);           // same DB
        paymentRepository.chargePayment(customerId, amount);   // same DB
        // If payment fails → everything rolls back automatically
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class MicroserviceOrderService {

    // In microservices, this DOES NOT work!
    public void placeOrder(String customerId, String productId,
                            BigDecimal amount) {
        orderClient.createOrder(customerId, productId);     // Service A, DB A
        inventoryClient.reserveStock(productId);             // Service B, DB B
        paymentClient.charge(customerId, amount);            // Service C, DB C
        // If payment fails → order and inventory are ALREADY COMMITTED
        // @Transactional cannot reach DB A and DB B to roll them back
    }
}
```

```text
// Monolith: one @Transactional → one rollback → all or nothing
// Microservices: three services, three databases, three separate commits
// Payment failure → order committed, stock reserved, no way to auto-rollback
```

### What is the Saga pattern?

A Saga models the operation as an ordered series of local transactions T1, T2, T3, each committed immediately in its own service. For each forward step with a side effect, you define a compensating transaction C that semantically reverses it.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderSagaDefinition {

    // Forward steps and their compensations:
    // T1: Create Order       → C1: Cancel Order
    // T2: Reserve Stock       → C2: Release Stock
    // T3: Charge Payment      → C3: Refund Payment
    // T4: Schedule Delivery   → (non-compensatable, runs last)

    // If T3 (Payment) fails:
    // Run C2: Release Stock
    // Run C1: Cancel Order
    // Do NOT run T4: Delivery never starts
}
```

### How does the orchestrator drive the Saga?

An orchestrator owns the Saga state machine. It invokes each step, records the state, and on failure drives compensations in reverse order.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class OrderSagaOrchestrator {

    private final OrderClient orderClient;
    private final InventoryClient inventoryClient;
    private final PaymentClient paymentClient;
    private final DeliveryClient deliveryClient;
    private final SagaStateRepository sagaRepo;

    public OrderSagaOrchestrator(OrderClient orderClient,
                                   InventoryClient inventoryClient,
                                   PaymentClient paymentClient,
                                   DeliveryClient deliveryClient,
                                   SagaStateRepository sagaRepo) {
        this.orderClient = orderClient;
        this.inventoryClient = inventoryClient;
        this.paymentClient = paymentClient;
        this.deliveryClient = deliveryClient;
        this.sagaRepo = sagaRepo;
    }

    public String executeSaga(String customerId, String productId,
                               BigDecimal amount) {
        String sagaId = "SAGA-" + System.currentTimeMillis();
        sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "STARTED");

        try {
            // T1: Create Order
            String orderId = orderClient.createOrder(customerId, productId);
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "ORDER_CREATED");

            // T2: Reserve Stock
            inventoryClient.reserveStock(productId, orderId);
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "STOCK_RESERVED");

            // T3: Charge Payment
            paymentClient.charge(customerId, amount, orderId);
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "PAYMENT_CHARGED");

            // T4: Schedule Delivery (non-compensatable, runs last)
            deliveryClient.schedule(orderId);
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "COMPLETED");

            return orderId;

        } catch (PaymentFailedException e) {
            // Payment failed: compensate in reverse order
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "COMPENSATING");
            inventoryClient.releaseStock(productId);  // C2
            orderClient.cancelOrder(customerId);        // C1
            sagaRepo.save(sagaId, "COMPENSATED");
            throw new OrderFailedException(
                    "Payment failed, order compensated: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
// Happy path: T1 → T2 → T3 → T4 → COMPLETED
// Payment fails: T1 → T2 → T3(fail) → C2 → C1 → COMPENSATED
// End state: order CANCELLED, stock released, no money charged
```

### Why must compensations be idempotent?

The Saga may retry a compensation after a transient failure. Releasing stock twice or refunding twice must be safe.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class InventoryService {

    private final InventoryRepository inventoryRepo;

    public InventoryService(InventoryRepository inventoryRepo) {
        this.inventoryRepo = inventoryRepo;
    }

    // Idempotent compensation: safe to call multiple times
    public void releaseStock(String productId, String orderId) {
        var reservation = inventoryRepo.findByOrderId(orderId);
        if (reservation == null || reservation.isReleased()) {
            return; // Already released or never reserved, no-op
        }
        reservation.release();
        inventoryRepo.save(reservation);
    }
}
```

```text
// First call:  reservation exists, not released → release it
// Second call: reservation exists, already released → no-op
// No reservation: orderId not found → no-op
// Safe to retry without double-releasing
```

### Why must non-compensatable steps run last?

Some steps cannot be undone. Once you physically ship an item, you cannot un-ship it. That is why delivery scheduling always comes after payment succeeds.

```java
// Step ordering:
// T1: Create Order       (compensatable: cancel)
// T2: Reserve Stock       (compensatable: release)
// T3: Charge Payment      (compensatable: refund) ← PIVOT POINT
// T4: Schedule Delivery   (non-compensatable: cannot un-ship)

// T4 only runs after T3 succeeds
// If T3 fails → compensate T2 and T1 → delivery never starts
// If T4 fails after T3 succeeds → forward recovery (retry delivery)
```

### How does choreography compare to orchestration for Sagas?

| Aspect | Orchestration | Choreography |
|---|---|---|
| Coordinator | Central orchestrator | None (event-driven) |
| Visibility | State machine shows exact state | Must trace events across services |
| Compensation | Orchestrator drives in order | Each service handles failure events |
| Coupling | Services coupled to orchestrator | Services coupled to event types |
| Complexity | One place for flow logic | Flow scattered across services |
| Best for | Complex flows with compensations | Simple, independent fan-outs |

```java
// Choreography version (no coordinator):
// Order Service: creates order → publishes OrderCreated event
// Inventory Service: hears OrderCreated → reserves stock → publishes StockReserved
// Payment Service: hears StockReserved → charges → publishes PaymentCharged
// If Payment fails → publishes PaymentFailed
// Inventory Service: hears PaymentFailed → releases stock
// Order Service: hears PaymentFailed → cancels order

// Problem: who tracks the state? Nobody.
// Problem: compensation order is implicit and fragile
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

The most common mistake is trying to use two-phase commit across REST services.

```java
// WRONG: trying distributed 2PC across REST services
// 1. Begin distributed transaction
// 2. orderService.prepare()     → "PREPARED"
// 3. inventoryService.prepare() → "PREPARED"
// 4. paymentService.prepare()   → timeout!
// 5. Coordinator stuck: rollback the others? What if they already committed?
// 2PC across REST is blocking, slow, and usually not supported

// CORRECT: Saga with compensating transactions
// 1. T1: createOrder() → committed
// 2. T2: reserveStock() → committed
// 3. T3: chargePayment() → FAILED
// 4. C2: releaseStock() → compensated
// 5. C1: cancelOrder() → compensated
// Eventually consistent, no blocking, no coordinator lock
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer uses the transactional outbox to ensure each step and its event are written atomically. They keep explicit Saga state for crash recovery. They prefer orchestration for complex flows with compensations because it is observable and testable as a unit.

```java
// Senior checklist for production Sagas:
// 1. Each step: local commit + outbox entry in same transaction
// 2. Compensations: idempotent and retryable
// 3. Non-compensatable steps: ordered last (after pivot point)
// 4. Saga state: persisted for crash recovery and observability
// 5. Orchestrator: state machine (Spring State Machine / Temporal)
// 6. Timeouts: each step has a timeout, expired steps trigger compensation
```
